  {
    "id": "1793153666318",
    "name": "Olivia Tiedemann’s Psychosis Crab Cakes",
    "image": "https://hips.hearstapps.com/hmg-prod/images/86928c68-d5c1-4f6c-b6bb-08ecea27bbde.jpg?crop=0.5xw:1xh;0.226xw,0xh&resize=1200:*",
    "categories": [
      "seafood"
    ],
    "prepTime": "30 min",
    "cookTime": "50 min",
    "ingredients": [
      "1 lb. fresh jumbo lump",
      "crabmeat (not canned; will",
      "also accept king crab leg",
      "meat)",
      "Juice of 2 lemons",
      "1/4 cup mayonnaise",
      "1 large egg",
      "1 large egg yolk",
      "2 garlic cloves, grated",
      "2 tbsp. chopped flat-leaf parsley leaves",
      "1 tbsp. chopped cilantro leaves",
      "1 tbsp. plus 1 tsp. Dijon mustard",
      "1 tsp. Old Bay seasoning",
      "Kosher salt",
      "1 cup plus 2 Tbsp.",
      "panko, plus more for",
      "coating",
      "3 tbsp. unsalted",
      "butter",
      "1 tbsp. neutral",
      "oil, such as avocado or",
      "vegetable",
      "Corn mayo, for serving (optional)",
      "Kernels from 4 ears of sweet corn",
      "1 garlic clove",
      "1 tsp. freshly squeezed lemon juice",
      "Kosher Salt"
    ],
    "instructions": [
      "Line a small tray with paper towels. Drain your crabmeat in a fine-mesh strainer, put it on the tray, and put another layer of paper towels on top—like a diaper ready to receive all the strange seafood juices from the crab that we do not want<br>in the crab cakes. Gently press the crab to get all the juice the fuck out of here.",
      "In a large bowl, combine the lemon juice, mayo, egg, egg yolk, garlic, parsley, cilantro, mustard, Old Bay, and a big, fat pinch of salt. Whisk thoroughly to blend well.",
      "Add your drained, diapered, and otherwise DRIED crab and ½ cup plus 2 tablespoons of the panko and, using a rubber spatula, gently toss to combine so you don’t break up the meat too much. Cover the bowl with plastic wrap and throw it in the fridge to chill for about 20 minutes or overnight.",
      "When you’re ready to cook the cakes, spread the remaining ½ cup of panko on a sheet pan.",
      "Use your hands to form the crab mixture into nice hockey puck–sized disks and arrange them on top of the panko-sprinkled tray. Press them gently into the crumbs, then flip them so they catch more crumbs on the other side. We want a kinda half-assed coating—this is about crabs today, not crumbs.",
      "Find yourself a good pan. Maybe a nice 12-inch stainless steel sauté pan. Idk, don’t flirt with me. Heat it over medium-low heat. Combine the butter and oil in the pan, giving it a swirl as the butter melts. Lay your crab cakes in the pan, leaving at least an inch of space between them. Work in batches. Don’t be overcrowding your pan. How old are you? I shouldn’t have to tell you this.",
      "Cook until the first side of the cakes is golden brown, 3 to 4 minutes. Flip and cook the second side until it’s golden brown as well, another 3 to 4 minutes. The slow approach is what helps the cakes cook through in the center before they brown too much on the outside. Be patient; you will be rewarded. Transfer the cooked cakes to a cooling rack and repeat with the remaining cakes. Serve with corn mayo, if you know what’s good for you.",
      "In a blender, combine the corn, garlic, and water. Blend until almost smooth—it’ll pretty much never get totally smooth in a blender. It’s fine; it doesn’t matter; don’t get me started.",
      "Set a fine-mesh strainer over a small saucepan and pour the blended corn mixture into it. Use a rubber spatula to scrape out the blender jar and to press all the liquid out of the corn glop and into the pan. What’s left behind in the strainer is corn trash. Throw it away. Get it out of here.",
      "Bring the corn water to a boil over medium-high heat. Reduce the heat to medium- low and continuously whisk until the mixture has reduced and thickened to the consistency of mayo, about 8 minutes. (By “continuously whisk” I don’t mean standing over the pot sweating into your corn water like a freak. Just whisk it actively enough so that it reduces evenly without burning.) Add the lemon juice and season with salt."
    ],
    "servings": "8 serving(s)",
    "description": "Fresh off the release of her new cookbook \"Eat (Like) The Rich,\" chef and creator Olivia Tiedemann joined us in the Delish Test Kitchen to teach us how to make her Psychosis Crab Cakes.",
    "videos": [
      {
        "url": "https://mos-streaming.hearst.io/669933d6-fe35-4915-b493-6d5d00b45abd/video_16x9_1080p_h264_hd.mp4",
        "title": "Olivia Tiedemann's Psychosis Crab Cakes",
        "thumbnailUrl": "https://hips.hearstapps.com/hmg-prod/images/2417e3fb-5eb7-43c2-b6ba-65fb2e3496db.jpg?crop=0.889xw:1xh;0.056xw,0xh"
      }
    ]
  },
